    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_756594889332ba797b574f0f0729b52610343c00f73573ec77dfc628844d1ebc",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The retained prime sums form variance-balanced scale increments whose dependence is indexed by spatial resolution: on the 1/log T mesh, distant points have decorrelated block histories, whereas nearby points share the early blocks and separate only at later scales. The second-moment argument exploits both sides of this geometry: far-pair probabilities factor and dominate numerically, while the larger joint tails of close pairs are offset by their scarcity. A rooted Gaussian tree with K-1 generations makes the shared-scale mechanism explicit, but only as an approximate model for this fixed coarse graining; the exceptional first and last blocks are removed, and the proof still owes the quantitative one- and two-point deviation estimates.",
      "parent_sources": [
        {
          "source_object_id": "mathia_source_aceb23c2d126f822c51e31545bc0fb0d296b142bf2c132ef98d222d80066f91c",
