    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_0e74f58967cf45b5674f7d0b166d33d8c2cc8bfce4b17552f63869b48fbe5ce0",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Height averaging first filters a prime Dirichlet polynomial to diagonal multiplicative data; the resulting Bessel Euler product then separates into a quadratic Gaussian factor and a controlled higher-order remainder, yielding finite-range joint Gaussian moments with distance-dependent covariance. A high-probability cutoff prevents exceptional values from dominating Taylor resummation, so those moment comparisons pass to one- and two-point Fourier-Laplace transforms. Smoothed contour inversion converts the transforms into exceedance probabilities and far-pair factorization; on a fine mesh, this far-pair estimate together with a separate crude near-pair bound makes the second moment asymptotic to the squared first moment and hence forces at least one simultaneous high point for the retained prime blocks.",
